<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Lets a cook timer break through Focus and Do Not Disturb.

		Without this, iOS silently downgrades `timeSensitive` to `active` — the
		notification still arrives, but it waits politely behind a Focus mode,
		which for a timer means the food is ruined before anyone is told. The
		log line to look for is `interruption-level: active` where you asked for
		timeSensitive, and `timeSensitiveSetting: NotSupported` in the app's
		notification settings. Nothing else announces it.

		This one Apple grants on request in the developer portal — unlike
		critical alerts, which pierce the silent switch and are not given to
		cooking apps. A silenced phone therefore still relies on the burst and
		the in-page alarm, and the UI should say so rather than let someone
		discover it with a burnt loaf.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.usernotifications.time-sensitive</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
